Wait, let me reconsider. The `coerce` method is used to handle `10 - Distance.new(3)` by allowing the Integer to delegate to Distance's subtraction logic.

```ruby
class Distance
  attr_reader :meters

  def initialize(meters)
    @meters = meters
  end

  def -(other)
    if other.is_a?(Distance)
      Distance.new(@meters - other.meters)
    else
      Distance.new(@meters - other)
    end
  end

  def ==(other)
    return false unless other.is_a?(Distance)
    @meters == other.meters
  end

  def coerce(other)
    [Distance.new(other), self]
  end
end
```

The `coerce` method is the key here. When Ruby encounters `10 - Distance.new(3)`, it tries to call `-(10)` on Distance, which will fail. Then Ruby calls `coerce` on the Distance object with the Integer `10`, which returns `[Distance.new(10), self]`. Then Ruby performs `Distance.new(10) - self`, which uses our `#-` method and gives the correct result.

